#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# around

#% text
# `around(x, dx)` represents the value `x` with the standard uncertainty `dx`.

#% text
# `around(list)` gives the mean of the list with its standard deviation as the uncertainty.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Arithmetic and functions propagate the uncertainty to first order, assuming independent arguments: f(x +- d) is f(x) +- |f'(x)| d.

#% item
# The centre and the uncertainty are machine numbers.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
around(1/2, 0.01)
#% output
#= around(0.5, 0.01)

#% cell
around(1.0, 0.1) + around(2.0, 0.2)
#% output
#= around(3.0, 0.223606797749979)

#% cell
sin(around(1.0, 0.1))
#% output
#= around(0.8414709848078965, 0.05403023058681398)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% cell
gamma(around(2.5, 0.01))
#% output
#= around(1.329340388179137, 0.009347345216260855)

#% cell
around(2.0, 0.1)^2
#% output
#= around(4.0, 0.4)

#% cell
around([1.1, 0.9, 1.0, 1.2])
#% output
#= around(1.05, 0.12909944487358055)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The mean of uncertain values carries their uncertainties:

#% cell
mean([around(1.0, 0.1), around(2.0, 0.2)])
#% output
#= around(1.5, 0.1118033988749895)

#% text
# Comparisons are decided when the spreads do not overlap:

#% cell
around(1.0, 0.1) < around(3.0, 0.5)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# Step functions give their value when it is the same over the spread:

#% cell
floor(around(2.5, 0.1))
#% output
#= 2

#% text
# A quantity with an uncertainty, absolute or relative:

#% cell
around(quantity(-34.78, "Newtons"), scaled(0.04))
#% output
#= quantity(around(-34.78, 1.3912), "Newtons")

#% text
# Functions without a closed-form derivative propagate by a difference quotient:

#% cell
factorial(around(2.0, 0.01))
#% output
#= around(2.0, 0.018455686701918845)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `interval`, `centered_interval`, `mean`, `std_dev`
